"Full article: Folding of DNA origamis (https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/21553769.2013.768556)\nciteturn36search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.6 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago;   * View PDF (open in a new window) PDF (open in a new window) ... A model of DNA origami folding ... In the following we describe a simplified version of a model (Arbona et al. ... Modelling the folding of DNA origami .\n\nImage: Publication Cover\n\nFrontiers in Life Science Volume 6, 2012 - Issue 1-2\n\nSubmit an article Journal homepage\n\nFree access\n\n854\n\nViews\n\n4\n\nCrossRef citations to date\n\n0\n\nAltmetric\n\nListen\n\nPrimary Research\n\n# Folding of DNA origamis\n\nJean-Michel Arbona CBMN, UMR 5248, CNRS, 2 r. R. Escarpit, 33600 Pessac, France\n\n,\n\nJean-Pierre Aimé CBMN, UMR 5248, CNRS, 2 r. R. Escarpit, 33600 Pessac, France\n\n&\n\nJuan Elezgaray CBMN, UMR 5248, CNRS, 2 r. R. Escarpit, 33600 Pessac, France Correspondence j.elezgaray@gmail.com\n\nPages 11-18 | Received 13 Nov 2012, Accepted 17 Jan 2013, Published online: 20 Mar 2013\n\n  * Cite this article\n  * https://doi.org/10.1080/21553769.2013.768556\n\n  * Full Article\n  * Figures & data\n  * References\n  * Citations\n  * Metrics\n  * Reprints & Permissions\n  * View PDF (open in a new window) PDF (open in a new window)\n\n## Abstract\n\nDNA based nanostructures built on a long single stranded DNA scaffold, known as DNA origamis, offer the possibility to organize various molecules at the nanometer scale in one pot experiments. The folding of the scaffold is guaranteed by the presence of short, single stranded DNA sequences (staples), which hold together separate regions of the scaffold. In this paper, we first consider simple structures made of three single-stranded oligonucleotides. Based on experimental (UV absorption) and numerical (replica exchange molecular dynamics simulations) data, we show that cooperativity is key to understand the thermodynamics of these constructions. In a second part, we derive a model of the annealing–melting properties of DNA origamis. The model captures important features such as the hysteresis between melting and annealing, as well as the dependence upon the topology of the scaffold. We also obtain temperature dependent average conformations which compare well to AFM images of quenched states of the partially folded origamis.\n\nKeywords:\n\n  * DNA origami\n  * folding\n  * modeling\n\n## Introduction\n\nNucleic acids are characterized by the predictability of their double-helical structure. The thermodynamic properties of simple DNA constructions are well described by the high specificity of Watson–Crick binding interactions. These are two major reasons of the success of DNA as an engineering material. In fact, DNA nanotechnology is emerging as a multifaceted tool, allowing the construction of a growing variety of structures, molecular motors and dynamical circuits, at the nanometer scale.\n\nA good example of the success of the rational design of complex nanostructures are DNA origamis (Rothemund Citation2006). These structures are formed in an annealing process where a long single stranded DNA (ssDNA) scaffold extracted from a virus (M13mp18) is folded with the help of a set of ∼200 short ssDNA strands, called staples. The resulting structure has a typical size of 100 nm. Locally, an origami is made of double stranded DNA (dsDNA) helices, where one of the strands corresponds to the scaffold. Planar and 3D structures are obtained as the superposition of DNA helices, with basically only one constraint: the staples should run from one helix to the other in such a way that mechanical deformations are minimized. This seemingly simple process has opened the way to the fabrication of a variety of nanostructures in 2D or 3D, acting as biosensors (Ke et al. Citation2008) or nanorobots (Gu et al. Citation2009; Lund et al. Citation2010; Wickham et al. Citation2011). DNA origamis also provide an easy tool to organize molecules such as proteins or carbon nanotubes (Maune et al. Citation2010) at the nanometer scale.\n\nHowever, despite these innovative realizations, the folding process of DNA origamis remains poorly understood. Open questions include the exact role of the staple connectivity, the differences in yield for very similar origamis (Rothemund Citation2006), or the factors that can influence the global stability of these nanostructures. In this paper, we show that topology is a key ingredient to understanding the stability of origami structures. We first discuss the properties of structures made of three ssDNA (called ‘small origamis’ in the following). Those can be considered as the building blocks of the usual DNA origamis, but can also be related to the biologically relevant Holliday junctions. We will show that both experimental and numerical data point to the importance of cooperativity to explain the stability properties of these particular structures. In a second part, we will discuss how the properties of these building blocks can explain some aspects of the annealing–melting properties (Arbona et al. Citation2012; Song et al. Citation2012) of DNA origamis.\n\n## 1. A model of DNA origami folding\n\nThe previous section considered the folding of simple DNA constructions that go beyond the simplest double stranded DNA structure. A visual inspection of Rothemund DNA origamis shows that small origamis can be considered as their building blocks. Therefore, we expect that the same physical mechanisms (cooperativity and topological effects) that explain the formation of small origamis will be present in the formation of larger origamis. In the following we describe a simplified version of a model (Arbona et al. Citation2011) that reflects some aspects of the annealing–melting properties of 2D flat origamis.\n\nThe DNA origami folding process can be generally described by a set of insertion reactions of the form\n\nwhere S _{ i } denotes the ith staple, and N(S _{ i }) represents a particular set of neighbour staples that influence the insertion of staple S _{ i }. EquationEquation (1) generalizes the idea developed in the previous section according to which the hybridization of a staple is dependent on the neighbour staples, but also on the topology of the binding.\n\nThe model aims to compute the probability p(S _{ i }, T) of having the staple S _{ i } folded at temperature T. We assume that at very high temperature T=T _{ h }, all the staples are unfolded: p(S _{ i }, T _{ h })=0 (in practice, T _{ h }=90°C). The model is recursive: p(S _{ i }, T+d T) is computed based on the knowledge of p(S _{ i }, T). The increment d T can be positive or negative: the algorithm starts from T _{ h }, the temperature decreases down to a value T _{ l }, then increases again. At any temperature T, the probability to observe the binding of a given staple S _{ i } depends upon the presence (or not) of neighbour staples. Therefore, for each staple a set of possible neighbour staples {N _{1}(S _{ i }), N _{2}(S _{ i }), …} needs to be defined. The model uses a locality assumption: only configurations N(S _{ i }) in a close neighbourhood of the staple S _{ i } of interest are considered.\n\nThe probability p(S _{ i }, T) for staple S _{ i } to be hybridized at temperature T is the solution of the coupled set of equations:\n\nHere, p(S _{ i }, T | N(S _{ i })) denotes the conditional probability to hybridize staple S _{ i } in the neighbourhood N(S _{ i }). EquationEquation (2) only determines the p(S _{ i }, T) probabilities but not the joint probabilities p(N(S _{ i }), T)=p(S _{ j }, …, T) to have at the same time the staples S _{ j }, …. that belong to N(S _{ i }). In Arbona et al. (2011), we use a strong correlation hypothesis, which can be simply stated by saying that any staple S _{ j } in the neighbourhood N(S _{ i }) of the S _{ i } staple that has a melting temperature T _{ m }(S _{ j })>T _{ m }(S _{ i }) is hybridized when S _{ i } binds to the scaffold.\n\nThis shows that the folding of each staple is strongly dependent on the existence of a nearby cluster of folded staples.\n\nWe have also shown that the predicted average structures are compatible with the AFM images obtained on quenched structures of the O1 origami. These experiments clearly need to be improved, both to quantify the influence of the experimental conditions (quenching the structure or controlling the temperature without quenching) and the interaction with the surface (Song et al. Citation2012). Extensions to 3D (Dietz et al. Citation2009; Douglas et al. Citation2009) and structures other than origami (Hansen et al. Citation2010) are envisioned, as well as tests at the single molecule level (FRET). The inclusion of kinetic effects is also another aspect we would like to include, particularly in view of the recent results reported by Dietz's group (Sobzac et al. Citation2012) showing the possibility to obtain almost defect-free origamis with very fast annealing protocols.\n\n### Acknowledgements\n\nThis work was partially supported by CNRS (PIR funding). Thanks to Carmelo di Primo for letting us use his equipment. Thanks to Jean-Louis Mergny for letting us use his equipment and to Thao Tran for the Origami O2.\n\n## References\n\n  * Arbona JM, Aimé JP, Elezgaray J. Forthcoming 2011. Cooperativity in the annealing of DNA origamis. Available from: http://arxiv.org/abs/1111.7130(open in a new window)\n\n(Open in a new window)Google Scholar\n\n  * Arbona , J M , Elezgaray , J and Aimé , J P . 2012 . Modelling the folding of DNA origami . Europhys Lett. , 100 : 28006 (doi:10.1209/0295-5075/100/28006(open in a new window))\n\n(Open in a new window)Google Scholar\n\n  * Dietz , H , Douglas , S and Shih , W . 2009 . Folding DNA into twisted and curved nanoscale shapes . Science. , 325 : 725 – 30 . (doi:10.1126/science.1174251(open in a new window))\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInterdisciplinary topics (https://ergodic.ugr.es/noticias/epl-highlights-2012.pdf)\nciteturn36search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.7 years ago; Crawled: 13.7 years ago; Modelling the folding of DNA origami ... Arbona, J. ... We have developed a model for the folding and melting processes of DNA origamis that is able to reproduce accurately several thermodynamic quantities measurable from UV absorption experiments.\nInterdisciplinary topics\n\nModelling the folding of DNA origami\n\nJ. M. Arbona, J. Elezgaray and J. P. Aimé\n\n2012 EPL 100 28006\n\nDNA-based nanostructures built from a long single-stranded DNA scaffold, known as DNA origamis, are at the basis of many applications. Despite their widespread development, many basic questions concerning the mechanisms of formation of DNA origamis have not yet been addressed. For instance, the robustness of different designs against factors such as the internal topology, or the influence of the staple pattern, are handled empirically. We have developed a model for the folding and melting processes of DNA origamis that is able to reproduce accurately several thermodynamic quantities measurable from UV absorption experiments. This model incorporates not only the origami sequence but also its topology. We show that cooperativity is key to quantitatively understand the folding process. The model can also be used to design a new distribution of crossovers that increases the robustness of the DNA template, a necessary step for technological development.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinalDetailedSchedule14April (https://www2.cs.duke.edu/fnano/FNANOschedules/FNANO12schedule.pdf)\nciteturn36search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Modelling the folding of DNA origami  \n Jean Michel Arbona, Elezgaray Juan and Jean Pierre Aimé ... Global surface functionalization of DNA origami structures with PAMAM dendrimers\n  Jason Micklefield\n\n4:25 – 4:45 Invited: Scalable and Tunable Platforms for